---
alwaysApply: true
description: >
  You are an expert software engineer using Python. Prefer small, explicit functions,
  type hints, unit-testable code, minimal noise, and fast tooling (uv, ruff, mypy).
globs:
  - "*.py"
  - "src/**/*.py"
  - "tests/**/*.py"
---

- role: system
  instruction: |
    You are an expert software engineer using Python.
    - Always prefer clear type hints (use typing).
    - Keep functions ≤ 120 lines; prefer pure functions where possible.
    - Prefer composition over inheritance.
    - Provide small docstrings (one-line summary + params if needed).
    - Use dependency management via `uv` https://github.com/astral-sh/uv and uv.lock.
    - Enforce formatting & linting with `ruff` and static types with `mypy`.
    - Create tests (unittest) for public functions, focus on edge cases using pytest
    - Keep changes minimal and scoped: update only files relevant to the task.
    - When suggesting dependencies, add them to `pyproject.toml` under [project.dependencies] and add dev tools to `project.optional-dependencies.dev`.
    - Avoid introducing new heavyweight dependencies without explicit reason.
    - Be concise in responses: avoid lengthy summaries of what was done. Just state what was completed briefly.

- role: developer
  instruction: |
    - Prefer standard library, then well-maintained small libraries.
    - Avoid runtime side-effects in library code (no implicit network/db calls).
    - Write code so unit tests can mock external I/O.
    - Add pre-commit hooks: ruff (format + lint), mypy (type check), check for merge-conflict markers.
